{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013NvqYophQ3xwQfLbyru7bj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UZRBarogVyMfScrEJKhG3Z","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/docs/paper/sections/analysis.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10690,"cache_read_input_tokens":16119,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10690,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":35,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FVX8NunzF4Uy4XCLQ2hvVd","session_id":"8ba2873e-73c0-42d5-b773-116ccfe0b545","uuid":"de58f8a3-df19-4ef7-90f8-4c4f3020f97a"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UZRBarogVyMfScrEJKhG3Z","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Analysis}\n2\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n3\t\n4\t\\subsection{Circular Evaluation and Its Implications}\n5\t\\label{sec:circular}\n6\t\n7\tThe structural RF's 0.995 macro-F1 (\\S\\ref{sec:main_results}) is a predictable consequence of feature--label overlap, not a detection achievement.\n8\tThe 30 structural features include repetition ratios and file operation counts---the same signals $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ consume to produce silver labels.\n9\tThis score therefore represents an upper bound on LF reverse-engineering fidelity (a feature overlap ablation in \\S\\ref{sec:feature_overlap} quantifies this decomposition).\n10\t\n11\tThree settings break this circularity: (1)~cross-coding-agent transfer (GB 0.967 on OH, where cmd\\_rep fires on 5.4\\% vs.\\ 65.5\\% of windows---LF memorization cannot explain this); (2)~instance isolation (removing 284 shared SWE-bench tasks changes cross-agent F1 by ${<}$0.1\\,pp, Table~\\ref{tab:instance_isolation}); (3)~gold validation (GB 0.839 vs.\\ MV 0.798, $p{<}0.001$; \\S\\ref{sec:gold_set}).\n12\tIn-domain structural metrics should still be interpreted as LF-agreement scores.\n13\t\n14\t\\subsection{Feature Overlap Ablation}\n15\t\\label{sec:feature_overlap}\n16\t\n17\tWe partition the 30 features into 11 \\emph{LF-overlapping} and 19 \\emph{residual} (Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_feature_overlap}).\n18\tOn all OH windows, 11 LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent F1---exceeding the full model by 1.0\\,pp---the opposite of what leakage would predict; this reverses on conf${\\geq}$2 ($-$0.71\\,pp, n.s.), where residual features aid noise filtering.\n19\tResidual features collapse to 0.566 cross-agent (0.952 in-domain), confirming agent-specific noise.\n20\tA leave-one-group-out ablation localizes the signal: removing cmd\\_rep drops cross-agent F1 by 40.6\\,pp; file\\_diff by 30.8\\,pp; outcome\\_bp by only 0.2\\,pp.\n21\t\n22\t\\subsection{Additional Analysis}\n23\t\\label{sec:why_tlm_fail}\n24\t\n25\t\\paragraph{Error analysis.}\n26\t\\label{sec:error_analysis}\n27\tThe probe misclassifies 7.2\\% of SWE-Agent windows; cross-coding-agent, the probe errs on 19.9\\% of OH windows vs.\\ 0.51\\% for GB---a 40$\\times$ gap with near-disjoint errors (Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_error_analysis}).\n28\t\n29\t\\paragraph{Outcome backpropagation.}\n30\t\\label{sec:ob_analysis}\n31\t$\\lambda_3$ contributes 33.5\\% of conf${\\geq}$2 labels while agreeing 100\\% with 2-LF labels where both are defined; removing it costs 7.7\\,pp (Appendix~\\ref{sec:app_ob_analysis}).\n32\t\n33\t\\paragraph{GB vs.\\ RF deployment trade-off.}\n34\tGB leads RF by 5.5\\,pp on all-windows cross-agent transfer (0.967 vs.\\ 0.912), but RF is more robust on conf${\\geq}$2 (0.996 vs.\\ 0.992), under 2-LF label degradation ($-$1.8\\,pp vs.\\ $-$12\\,pp), and in multi-seed variance (${\\pm}0.000$ vs.\\ ${\\pm}0.002$).\n35\tPractitioners should prefer GB for broad deployment and RF for high-confidence or label-degraded settings."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FVX8NunzF4Uy4XCLQ2hvVd","session_id":"8ba2873e-73c0-42d5-b773-116ccfe0b545","uuid":"8026a585-37ca-4496-a2ec-c106c3527b64","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:20:30.301Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013NvqYophQ3xwQfLbyru7bj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Eqf4Ntqo2uNmqDjXiaVL2S","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/docs/paper/sections/related_work.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10690,"cache_read_input_tokens":16119,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10690,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":35,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FVX8NunzF4Uy4XCLQ2hvVd","session_id":"8ba2873e-73c0-42d5-b773-116ccfe0b545","uuid":"d0bef409-bb51-4043-b2be-51f7b22c7493"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Eqf4Ntqo2uNmqDjXiaVL2S","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Related Work}\n2\t\\label{sec:related_work}\n3\t\n4\t\\paragraph{Process Supervision and Self-Reflection.}\n5\tPRMs assign step-level quality scores \\citep{xi2025agentprm,choudhury2025prm,fan2026agentrm}, building on process supervision \\citep{lightman2024letsverify,uesato2022processfeedback}, with benchmarks revealing long-horizon degradation \\citep{wang2026planrewardbench,li2026toolprmbench}.\n6\tSelf-reflection methods \\citep{shinn2023reflexion,yao2023tot,zhou2024lats} prompt agents to diagnose failures, but LLMs cannot reliably self-correct without external feedback \\citep{huang2024selfcorrect,kamoi2024selfcorrection}.\n7\tWe address a complementary question---\\emph{when} is the agent stuck---using external structural signals at window level, targeting real-time monitoring ($<$5\\,ms) without outcome labels.\n8\t\n9\t\\paragraph{Weak Supervision, Noisy Labels, and Label Leakage.}\n10\tData programming \\citep{ratner2016dataprogramming,ratner2019snorkel} enables dataset creation through labeling functions, extended via multi-task aggregation \\citep{ratner2019snorkelmetal}, triplet methods \\citep{fu2020flyingsquid}, sequential HMMs \\citep{safranchik2020linkedhmm}, multi-resolution LFs \\citep{sala2019multiresolution}, and benchmarked by WRENCH \\citep{zhang2021wrench}.\n11\tUnlike \\citeauthor{safranchik2020linkedhmm}'s NER results, temporal label models do not improve over majority vote in our low-conflict 3-LF regime (\\S\\ref{sec:temporal_label_models}).\n12\tOur silver labels connect to noisy label learning \\citep{han2018coteaching,li2020dividemix}; the structural feature--label overlap instantiates label leakage \\citep{kaufman2012leakage} and Goodhart's Law \\citep{manheim2018goodhart}, mitigated through cross-coding-agent evaluation, a gold set (\\S\\ref{sec:gold_set}), and active learning \\citep{settles2009active} for future expansion.\n13\t\n14\t\\paragraph{Agent Benchmarks and Monitoring.}\n15\tAgent evaluation spans diverse environments---general tasks \\citep{liu2024agentbench}, web navigation \\citep{zhou2024webarena}, interactive coding \\citep{yang2024intercode}, tool use \\citep{qin2024toolllm}, and debugging \\citep{tian2024debugbench}---creating demand for scalable monitoring.\n16\t\\citet{orlanski2026slopcodebench} document coding agent degradation; \\citet{chen2025monitoring} show sequence-level context is necessary; \\citet{kale2025weaktostrong} show weak monitors can oversee stronger agents; and \\citet{cemri2025multiagentfail,zhu2025agentfail} catalog systematic failure modes.\n17\tGuardrail frameworks \\citep{rebedea2023nemo,xiang2024guardagent} address safety but not progress.\n18\t\n19\t\\paragraph{Anomaly Detection, Process Mining, and Compute Efficiency.}\n20\tStagnation detection relates to sequential anomaly detection \\citep{chandola2009anomaly}, including deep log analysis \\citep{du2017deeplog,meng2019loganomaly} and runtime verification \\citep{leucker2009runtime}.\n21\tCycle detectors \\citep{george2025cycledetection,pipis2025reasoningloop} target structural repetition but miss semantically diverse stagnation; \\citet{liu2026trajad} train trajectory verifiers for broader anomaly detection.\n22\tOur structural features draw on process mining \\citep{vanderaalst2016processmining}; the success of hand-crafted features parallels classical findings for narrow detection tasks \\citep{liu2008isolationforest}.\n23\tEarly-exit architectures \\citep{teerapittayanon2016branchynet,schwartz2020righttool} and curriculum learning \\citep{bengio2009curriculum} reduce computation by adapting effort to instance difficulty---analogous to stagnation detection, where the goal is terminating unproductive agent computation.\n24\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FVX8NunzF4Uy4XCLQ2hvVd","session_id":"8ba2873e-73c0-42d5-b773-116ccfe0b545","uuid":"7c5b58e7-d4e5-4c64-9358-bfdb9b413062","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:20:30.654Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0122mxu2HhUodNSzdKWUCs5D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012fjx9twZ6BgNajHpbiWqBs","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/docs/paper/sections/conclusion.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2492,"cache_read_input_tokens":26809,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2492,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":37,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FVX8NunzF4Uy4XCLQ2hvVd","session_id":"8ba2873e-73c0-42d5-b773-116ccfe0b545","uuid":"2af34243-021c-4166-8c67-4a6aa24cd343"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012fjx9twZ6BgNajHpbiWqBs","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Conclusion}\n2\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n3\t\n4\tWe studied progress stagnation detection in LLM coding agents, comparing structural features, neural probes, and LLM judges across deployment regimes via weak supervision.\n5\tThree findings emerge.\n6\tFirst, a deployment-regime trade-off: in zero-data cross-coding-agent transfer, structural features outperform probes by 36.9\\,pp and the best LLM judge by 16.8\\,pp (0.967 vs.\\ 0.799); with fine-tuning, the probe surpasses structural features on all windows (0.976 vs.\\ 0.967) but not on conf${\\geq}$2 (0.976 vs.\\ 0.992).\n7\tSecond, LF temporal dynamics are agent-specific: cmd\\_rep leads stagnation on SWE-Agent ($-1.87$ steps) but lags on OpenHands ($+3.32$); label version bias was the dominant transfer bottleneck.\n8\tThird, five label models all fail to improve over majority vote---LF quantity, not aggregation, is the bottleneck.\n9\tPractitioners should use structural features ($<$5\\,ms, no GPU) for zero-data deployment and invest in probe fine-tuning when annotation budget permits.\n10\tGold validation ($N{=}505$, three annotators) confirms the structural advantage: GB achieves 0.839 gold macro-F1 vs.\\ 0.798 for MV labels ($p{<}0.001$).\n11\tFuture work includes non-SE domains, online integration, and scaling to ${>}$10 LFs.\n12\t\n13\t\\paragraph{Reproducibility.}\n14\tCode, silver labels, and trained models will be released upon acceptance; all trajectories derive from public SWE-bench runs \\citep{jimenez2024swebench}.\n15\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FVX8NunzF4Uy4XCLQ2hvVd","session_id":"8ba2873e-73c0-42d5-b773-116ccfe0b545","uuid":"32695341-d1e7-4d92-b53c-20c68a416ad7","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:20:34.477Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0122mxu2HhUodNSzdKWUCs5D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NS7endqRNAqTuqxbHkNKaR","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/docs/paper/sections/limitations.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2492,"cache_read_input_tokens":26809,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2492,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":37,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FVX8NunzF4Uy4XCLQ2hvVd","session_id":"8ba2873e-73c0-42d5-b773-116ccfe0b545","uuid":"1fb11c3c-455b-4c61-9e03-2a7c7072263f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NS7endqRNAqTuqxbHkNKaR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\label{sec:limitations}\n2\t\n3\t\\paragraph{Scope.}\n4\tWe evaluate two software engineering coding agents (SWE-Agent and OpenHands) on SWE-bench.\n5\tCross-coding-agent transfer is demonstrated between these two agents; generalization to non-coding agent domains---web navigation, data analysis, multi-agent collaboration---remains untested.\n6\t\n7\t\\paragraph{Silver labels.}\n8\tSilver-label evaluation dominates this study; while a 505-window gold set provides partial non-circular validation (\\S\\ref{sec:gold_set}), the primary metrics in Tables~1--5 remain silver-evaluated, and the structural RF's 0.995 macro-F1 reflects LF-reconstructibility rather than ground-truth detection accuracy.\n9\tModerate inter-annotator agreement (Fleiss' $\\kappa{=}0.39$) reflects the inherent subjectivity of stagnation boundaries.\n10\t\n11\t\\paragraph{Labeling function count.}\n12\tThree LFs produce insufficient inter-LF conflict (95.1\\% agreement at conf${\\geq}$2) for any label model---temporal or generative---to demonstrate value; scaling to ${>}$10 LFs with higher disagreement is needed.\n13\t\n14\t\\paragraph{No online evaluation.}\n15\tAll results are offline; no intervention or real-time deployment evaluation has been conducted.\n16\t\n17\t\\paragraph{Label version sensitivity.}\n18\tCross-coding-agent transfer results are sensitive to label quality: a single embedding version mismatch shifted the OH stagnation rate from 6.56\\% to 21.4\\% and improved 5\\% fine-tuning F1 from 0.570 to 0.850.\n19\tThis sensitivity suggests that deploying the pipeline on new agents requires careful label auditing before conclusions about transfer difficulty can be drawn.\n20\t\n21\t\\paragraph{Outcome dependence.}\n22\tThe outcome backpropagation labeling function ($\\lambda_3$) requires task outcome, which is unavailable during inference.\n23\t\n24\t\\paragraph{Code and data.}\n25\tCode, data, and trained models are anonymous during review and will be released upon acceptance.\n26\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FVX8NunzF4Uy4XCLQ2hvVd","session_id":"8ba2873e-73c0-42d5-b773-116ccfe0b545","uuid":"208056fc-f207-4367-b08d-c1aff93dc7bf","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:20:35.779Z"}
