{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DvHNnBxNYTcGiHkmCmvpj9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014d2sdmLz88YrxkF9TCz64H","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"From Detection to Intervention: Structural Progress Monitoring for Compute-Efficient LLM Agent Execution\"\n\nThe paper presents a framework for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories and using it for early termination. The approach:\n1. Designs 3 labeling functions (LFs): command repetition (cmd_rep), file diff, outcome backpropagation (OB)\n2. Generates silver labels via majority vote of LFs\n3. Trains a Gradient Boosted Tree (GB) on 30 structural features, plus neural embedding probes\n4. Evaluates cross-agent transfer (train on SWE-Agent, test on OpenHands zero-shot)\n5. Validates against 505-window human gold set (3 annotators, Fleiss κ=0.39)\n6. Simulates early termination policies\n\n## Key Claims\n1. Structural features achieve strong cross-agent transfer (0.967 F1), outperforming best LLM judge by ~18pp and zero-shot probes by 28-37pp\n2. LF temporal dynamics and label quality analysis\n3. TLM negative finding (three temporal label models fail to beat MV)\n4. Detection-to-intervention: ~32% compute waste, ~23% savings at k=3 with 91% task success\n\n## Related Work Coverage in Paper (Section 2)\n- Agent Failure Detection: CDCS, SpecRA, TrajAD, SlopCodeBench\n- Process Reward Models: AgentPRM, PRM Framework, ToolPRMBench, Trajectory Reward\n- Self-Reflection / LLM-as-Judge: Reflexion, LATS, Tree of Thoughts\n- Agent Monitoring: Seq Monitoring, Weak-to-Strong\n- Weak Supervision: Data Programming, Snorkel\n\n## Paper Structure\n- 10 pages, 12 tables, 3 qualitative trajectory descriptions\n- No figures (only tables and text descriptions)\n- References: 14 entries, several using placeholder-style author names (\"SlopCodeBench Authors\", \"CDCS Authors\", etc.)\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (novelty and positioning)\n3. **Weaknesses** (novelty and related work gaps) — be specific\n4. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Scores**: Soundness (1-4), Presentation (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Overall (1-6), Confidence (1-5)\n\nKey things to evaluate for NOVELTY:\n- Is \"semantic stagnation\" detection genuinely novel vs. existing cycle detection (CDCS, SpecRA) and process reward models (AgentPRM)?\n- The core method is a GBT on 30 hand-crafted features trained on weak-supervision labels — how novel is this technically?\n- The weak supervision pipeline (3 LFs → MV → train classifier) is standard Snorkel-style — what's the contribution beyond applying it to agent trajectories?\n- The early termination policy (k consecutive stagnation windows → terminate) is a very simple rule — is there novelty here?\n- Is the anti-circularity framework (multiple evaluation layers to address train-on-LF / eval-on-LF concern) a genuine methodological contribution?\n- Does the negative TLM result constitute meaningful contribution?\n\nKey things to evaluate for RELATED WORK:\n- Are key related works missing? Consider: runtime monitoring for agents, online learning for sequential decisions, bandit-based early stopping, active inference for agent monitoring\n- Several references use placeholder names (\"CDCS Authors\", \"SpecRA Authors\") — are these real papers?\n- Is the positioning vs. AgentPRM fair? The paper claims \"no outcome labels needed\" but OB is exactly outcome backpropagation\n- Is the positioning vs. weak supervision literature adequate? The paper uses standard Snorkel-style MV but doesn't discuss more sophisticated label models (which they then test as TLM)\n- Is there sufficient discussion of compute-efficient inference / early stopping in ML more broadly?\n\nKey things to evaluate for PRESENTATION:\n- No figures at all — a paper about trajectory patterns and Pareto curves has zero visualizations\n- 12 tables is very dense for a 10-page paper\n- Reference quality: placeholder author names\n\nWrite a thorough, fair review. Evaluate whether the contribution meets the bar for a top ML venue."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":44538,"cache_read_input_tokens":23725,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":44538},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8ba2873e-73c0-42d5-b773-116ccfe0b545","uuid":"d5c1af26-960b-45eb-bd79-063d6ef84f43"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a542328549621cae7","tool_use_id":"toolu_014d2sdmLz88YrxkF9TCz64H","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"From Detection to Intervention: Structural Progress Monitoring for Compute-Efficient LLM Agent Execution\"\n\nThe paper presents a framework for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories and using it for early termination. The approach:\n1. Designs 3 labeling functions (LFs): command repetition (cmd_rep), file diff, outcome backpropagation (OB)\n2. Generates silver labels via majority vote of LFs\n3. Trains a Gradient Boosted Tree (GB) on 30 structural features, plus neural embedding probes\n4. Evaluates cross-agent transfer (train on SWE-Agent, test on OpenHands zero-shot)\n5. Validates against 505-window human gold set (3 annotators, Fleiss κ=0.39)\n6. Simulates early termination policies\n\n## Key Claims\n1. Structural features achieve strong cross-agent transfer (0.967 F1), outperforming best LLM judge by ~18pp and zero-shot probes by 28-37pp\n2. LF temporal dynamics and label quality analysis\n3. TLM negative finding (three temporal label models fail to beat MV)\n4. Detection-to-intervention: ~32% compute waste, ~23% savings at k=3 with 91% task success\n\n## Related Work Coverage in Paper (Section 2)\n- Agent Failure Detection: CDCS, SpecRA, TrajAD, SlopCodeBench\n- Process Reward Models: AgentPRM, PRM Framework, ToolPRMBench, Trajectory Reward\n- Self-Reflection / LLM-as-Judge: Reflexion, LATS, Tree of Thoughts\n- Agent Monitoring: Seq Monitoring, Weak-to-Strong\n- Weak Supervision: Data Programming, Snorkel\n\n## Paper Structure\n- 10 pages, 12 tables, 3 qualitative trajectory descriptions\n- No figures (only tables and text descriptions)\n- References: 14 entries, several using placeholder-style author names (\"SlopCodeBench Authors\", \"CDCS Authors\", etc.)\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (novelty and positioning)\n3. **Weaknesses** (novelty and related work gaps) — be specific\n4. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Scores**: Soundness (1-4), Presentation (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Overall (1-6), Confidence (1-5)\n\nKey things to evaluate for NOVELTY:\n- Is \"semantic stagnation\" detection genuinely novel vs. existing cycle detection (CDCS, SpecRA) and process reward models (AgentPRM)?\n- The core method is a GBT on 30 hand-crafted features trained on weak-supervision labels — how novel is this technically?\n- The weak supervision pipeline (3 LFs → MV → train classifier) is standard Snorkel-style — what's the contribution beyond applying it to agent trajectories?\n- The early termination policy (k consecutive stagnation windows → terminate) is a very simple rule — is there novelty here?\n- Is the anti-circularity framework (multiple evaluation layers to address train-on-LF / eval-on-LF concern) a genuine methodological contribution?\n- Does the negative TLM result constitute meaningful contribution?\n\nKey things to evaluate for RELATED WORK:\n- Are key related works missing? Consider: runtime monitoring for agents, online learning for sequential decisions, bandit-based early stopping, active inference for agent monitoring\n- Several references use placeholder names (\"CDCS Authors\", \"SpecRA Authors\") — are these real papers?\n- Is the positioning vs. AgentPRM fair? The paper claims \"no outcome labels needed\" but OB is exactly outcome backpropagation\n- Is the positioning vs. weak supervision literature adequate? The paper uses standard Snorkel-style MV but doesn't discuss more sophisticated label models (which they then test as TLM)\n- Is there sufficient discussion of compute-efficient inference / early stopping in ML more broadly?\n\nKey things to evaluate for PRESENTATION:\n- No figures at all — a paper about trajectory patterns and Pareto curves has zero visualizations\n- 12 tables is very dense for a 10-page paper\n- Reference quality: placeholder author names\n\nWrite a thorough, fair review. Evaluate whether the contribution meets the bar for a top ML venue.","uuid":"d1efe963-2da8-4e3d-b895-6595dca9011a","session_id":"8ba2873e-73c0-42d5-b773-116ccfe0b545"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"From Detection to Intervention: Structural Progress Monitoring for Compute-Efficient LLM Agent Execution\"\n\nThe paper presents a framework for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories and using it for early termination. The approach:\n1. Designs 3 labeling functions (LFs): command repetition (cmd_rep), file diff, outcome backpropagation (OB)\n2. Generates silver labels via majority vote of LFs\n3. Trains a Gradient Boosted Tree (GB) on 30 structural features, plus neural embedding probes\n4. Evaluates cross-agent transfer (train on SWE-Agent, test on OpenHands zero-shot)\n5. Validates against 505-window human gold set (3 annotators, Fleiss κ=0.39)\n6. Simulates early termination policies\n\n## Key Claims\n1. Structural features achieve strong cross-agent transfer (0.967 F1), outperforming best LLM judge by ~18pp and zero-shot probes by 28-37pp\n2. LF temporal dynamics and label quality analysis\n3. TLM negative finding (three temporal label models fail to beat MV)\n4. Detection-to-intervention: ~32% compute waste, ~23% savings at k=3 with 91% task success\n\n## Related Work Coverage in Paper (Section 2)\n- Agent Failure Detection: CDCS, SpecRA, TrajAD, SlopCodeBench\n- Process Reward Models: AgentPRM, PRM Framework, ToolPRMBench, Trajectory Reward\n- Self-Reflection / LLM-as-Judge: Reflexion, LATS, Tree of Thoughts\n- Agent Monitoring: Seq Monitoring, Weak-to-Strong\n- Weak Supervision: Data Programming, Snorkel\n\n## Paper Structure\n- 10 pages, 12 tables, 3 qualitative trajectory descriptions\n- No figures (only tables and text descriptions)\n- References: 14 entries, several using placeholder-style author names (\"SlopCodeBench Authors\", \"CDCS Authors\", etc.)\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (novelty and positioning)\n3. **Weaknesses** (novelty and related work gaps) — be specific\n4. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Scores**: Soundness (1-4), Presentation (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Overall (1-6), Confidence (1-5)\n\nKey things to evaluate for NOVELTY:\n- Is \"semantic stagnation\" detection genuinely novel vs. existing cycle detection (CDCS, SpecRA) and process reward models (AgentPRM)?\n- The core method is a GBT on 30 hand-crafted features trained on weak-supervision labels — how novel is this technically?\n- The weak supervision pipeline (3 LFs → MV → train classifier) is standard Snorkel-style — what's the contribution beyond applying it to agent trajectories?\n- The early termination policy (k consecutive stagnation windows → terminate) is a very simple rule — is there novelty here?\n- Is the anti-circularity framework (multiple evaluation layers to address train-on-LF / eval-on-LF concern) a genuine methodological contribution?\n- Does the negative TLM result constitute meaningful contribution?\n\nKey things to evaluate for RELATED WORK:\n- Are key related works missing? Consider: runtime monitoring for agents, online learning for sequential decisions, bandit-based early stopping, active inference for agent monitoring\n- Several references use placeholder names (\"CDCS Authors\", \"SpecRA Authors\") — are these real papers?\n- Is the positioning vs. AgentPRM fair? The paper claims \"no outcome labels needed\" but OB is exactly outcome backpropagation\n- Is the positioning vs. weak supervision literature adequate? The paper uses standard Snorkel-style MV but doesn't discuss more sophisticated label models (which they then test as TLM)\n- Is there sufficient discussion of compute-efficient inference / early stopping in ML more broadly?\n\nKey things to evaluate for PRESENTATION:\n- No figures at all — a paper about trajectory patterns and Pareto curves has zero visualizations\n- 12 tables is very dense for a 10-page paper\n- Reference quality: placeholder author names\n\nWrite a thorough, fair review. Evaluate whether the contribution meets the bar for a top ML venue."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_014d2sdmLz88YrxkF9TCz64H","session_id":"8ba2873e-73c0-42d5-b773-116ccfe0b545","uuid":"e90c10d9-6f4d-49f2-a2c8-117c21decec7","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:20:43.995Z"}
