{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FVX8NunzF4Uy4XCLQ2hvVd","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me compile the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"From Detection to Intervention: Structural Progress Monitoring for Compute-Efficient LLM Agent Execution\"\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper studies stagnation detection in LLM coding agents using a weak supervision framework with three labeling functions (command repetition, file diff, outcome backpropagation) to generate silver labels over 205K+ sliding windows from SWE-Agent and OpenHands trajectories. The authors compare structural features (30-dim, trained with GB/RF/LR), a GRU-based neural embedding probe, and an LLM-as-judge baseline across in-domain and cross-agent transfer settings. The main claim is that lightweight structural features (no GPU, <5ms) achieve 0.967 macro-F1 in zero-data cross-agent transfer, outperforming neural probes by 36.9pp and the best LLM judge by 16.8pp, while a 505-window gold set partially validates these findings.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. Well-structured circularity analysis.** The paper is unusually transparent about the circular evaluation problem inherent in training on LF-derived labels and evaluating with LF-correlated features. Section 4 (circular evaluation caveat), Section 5.1 (circular evaluation subsection), and Section 5.2 (feature overlap ablation) systematically decompose this concern. The instance isolation experiment (Table A.5, delta < 0.1pp) is a particularly clean control.\n\n**S2. Cross-agent transfer is the right evaluation.** The SWE-Agent-to-OpenHands zero-shot transfer design is well-motivated and partially breaks circularity: cmd_rep fires on 65.5% of SWE-Agent windows but only 5.4% on OH, so the GB model cannot simply memorize LF firing patterns. The feature overlap ablation (Table A.7) showing that LF-overlapping features *alone* achieve 0.977 cross-agent F1 (exceeding the full 30-feature model) while residual features collapse to 0.566 is informative.\n\n**S3. Negative results are valuable.** The temporal label model results (Section 4.6, Appendix Table A.6) are a genuine contribution. Showing that five label models spanning temporal and generative approaches all fail to beat majority vote in the 3-LF regime is practically useful for practitioners. The root-cause analysis (insufficient inter-LF conflict, only 8 possible voting patterns) is convincing.\n\n**S4. Honest deployment trade-off framing.** The paper does not oversell: it clearly states that the probe surpasses structural features with fine-tuning (0.976 vs 0.967 on all windows) and delineates when each approach is appropriate. The data scaling curve (Figure 2) showing structural classifiers saturating at 10% training data versus the probe not plateauing at 100% is an informative practical finding.\n\n**S5. Thorough ablation design.** The paper includes encoder ablation (3 backbones, Appendix Table A.1), window size ablation (Table A.2), multi-seed stability (Table A.3), leave-one-group-out feature ablation (Table A.7), and prevalence-matched cmd_rep ablation (Table 2). The ablation coverage is above average for the venue.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. The 0.967 cross-agent F1 is fundamentally against silver labels, and the paper's framing obscures this.** The headline result (0.967 macro-F1) is evaluated against OH silver labels produced by the same family of labeling functions used to train the model. While the paper argues this partially breaks circularity because LF firing patterns differ across agents, the evaluation target remains LF-derived. The gold set (N=505, macro-F1=0.839) is the only non-circular evidence, and it shows a 12.8pp gap from the headline number. The abstract and introduction lead with \"0.967\" and present \"0.839\" as secondary validation, but from a soundness perspective the relationship should be inverted: the gold-validated performance is 0.839, and readers should treat this as the primary result. This framing choice inflates the perceived contribution.\n\n**W2. The gold set is underpowered for the claims it needs to support.** N=505 with Fleiss kappa=0.39 (fair agreement) is thin for validating a system that claims practical deployment readiness. The paper's own context mentions a power analysis showing minimum detectable effect of 6.2pp at 80% power -- yet the GB vs MV gold difference is only 4.2pp. The conf=1 subset comparison (GB 0.718 vs MV 0.709, p=0.035, Section 4.7 context) is described as \"suggestive but underpowered\" -- this is honest but undermines the gold set's ability to independently validate the core claims. More critically, probe models are evaluated on a SWE-only subset (N~335) due to missing OH embeddings, making the gold comparison between structural and probe methods on different subsets (structural on N=505, probe on N~335), which is methodologically unclean.\n\n**W3. Multiple experiments from the design are missing from the paper.** Cross-referencing the 14-experiment design with the paper's content reveals significant gaps:\n- **Experiment 1 (Early Termination Simulation)**: Not present in any section. The paper title promises \"Intervention\" and mentions \"early termination\" in the introduction and limitations, but no simulation results (k=3 operating point, 23% savings, 91% success preservation) appear anywhere. This is a major gap given the title.\n- **Experiment 2 (Compute Waste Audit)**: Not present. The 32% waste figure appears nowhere in the paper.\n- **Experiment 3 (Cost-Effectiveness Pareto)**: Not present. No Pareto analysis, no AUPF metric.\n- **Experiment 9 (Calibration Analysis)**: Not present. The gold ECE 0.082 vs silver 0.045 result and overconfidence discussion are absent.\n- **Experiment 10 (Gold Power Analysis)**: Not present as a formal analysis, though the underpowered nature is mentioned in passing.\n- **Experiment 11 (Human Ceiling Estimate)**: Not present. Mean annotator F1 of 0.849 is not reported.\n- **Experiment 12 (Stride Sensitivity)**: Not present. The stride-1 autocorrelation of 0.72 and its anticonservative effect on bootstrap CIs is not discussed. This is a serious statistical concern -- if stride-1 creates high autocorrelation, all bootstrap CIs throughout the paper may be anticonservative, and this is never acknowledged.\n- **Experiment 14 (Trajectory Visualization)**: Not present.\n\nThe absence of the early termination simulation is particularly damaging given the title's promise of \"Intervention.\" Without it, the paper is purely about detection with no validated intervention path.\n\n**W4. The 2-LF deployable configuration claim is undermined by the paper's own data.** The cmd_rep ablation (Table 2) shows that removing cmd_rep drops macro-F1 by 29.4pp. The paper notes that cmd_rep fires on only 5.4% of OH windows. The experiment design context indicates 2-LF conf>=2 coverage is only 2.8% on OH vs 18.7% on SWE-Agent. If outcome backpropagation (lambda_3) requires task outcome unavailable at inference (acknowledged in limitations), the deployable configuration is actually 2-LF, which is catastrophically weak. The paper does not adequately address what a realistic deployment looks like without lambda_3.\n\n**W5. Statistical tests may be invalid due to stride-1 autocorrelation.** The experiment design mentions stride-1 autocorrelation of 0.72. With overlapping windows (stride 1, window size 5), consecutive windows share 4 of 5 actions, creating massive dependence. All bootstrap confidence intervals, McNemar tests, and p-values throughout the paper assume (at least approximately) independent observations. An autocorrelation of 0.72 would dramatically inflate test statistics and narrow CIs. The paper never discusses this issue, yet it affects every statistical test reported (paired bootstrap in Table 1, McNemar in Table 2, gold set comparisons in Table A.8).\n\n**W6. The LLM-as-judge comparison is unfair.** The best LLM judge (five-shot-CoT, 0.799) is evaluated in-domain on SWE-Agent, while the structural GB (0.967) is evaluated cross-agent on OH -- both against silver labels. The paper explicitly notes \"the structural advantage holds even comparing the harder cross-agent setting against the LLM's easier in-domain regime\" (Section 4.2), but this is misleading: the LLM is evaluated on a different test set with different characteristics. A fair comparison would evaluate both methods on the same test set. The GPT-5.5 result has 22.4% parsing failures (408/1824 windows), and computing F1 only on parseable outputs introduces selection bias.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** Given that the title promises \"Intervention\" and mentions early termination repeatedly, where are the early termination simulation results? The experiment design includes a k=3 operating point saving 23% compute while preserving 91% task success. Was this experiment conducted? If so, why is it absent from the paper? If not, the title should be revised.\n\n**Q2.** How do you address the stride-1 autocorrelation (0.72) in your statistical tests? Consecutive windows share 4 of 5 actions, creating strong dependence. Have you verified that your bootstrap CIs remain valid under this dependence structure? A block bootstrap with block length >= 5 would be more appropriate. What happens to the GB vs MV gold-set p-value (reported as p<0.001) under a dependence-corrected test?\n\n**Q3.** What is the realistic deployable configuration? Lambda_3 requires task outcome (unavailable at inference). Lambda_1 fires on only 5.4% of OH windows. The 2-LF configuration has 2.8% conf>=2 coverage on OH. What stagnation detection coverage can a practitioner actually expect when deploying this system on a new agent without task outcomes?\n\n**Q4.** The gold set evaluates probe models on a SWE-only subset (N~335) but structural models on the full set (N=505). Why are OH embeddings missing? This prevents the most important comparison -- structural vs probe on the same gold examples including cross-agent transfer cases. Can you provide a scope-matched comparison?\n\n**Q5.** The feature overlap ablation shows LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent F1, exceeding the full model. This suggests residual features actively hurt cross-agent transfer. Have you tried training with only the 11 LF-overlapping features for deployment? What is the gold-set performance of this reduced model?\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The label polarity convention (0=stagnation, 1=progress) is counterintuitive for binary classification where the \"positive\" class is typically the phenomenon being detected. The error analysis (Appendix A.9) switches to \"stagnation-positive convention\" midway, creating confusion.\n\n- Table 1 footnote $^a$ notes that probe is evaluated on N=6,122 while structural models on N=150,652 (or N=43,376 for conf>=2). This sample size mismatch makes the comparison in the same table misleading without explicit caveat in the main text.\n\n- The paper uses \"coding agent\" and \"agent\" interchangeably but calls it \"cross-coding-agent transfer\" in a way that reads awkwardly. \"Cross-agent\" is clearer.\n\n- The Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B citation (zhang2025qwen3embedding) should be verified -- Qwen3 embedding models may not have existed under this name at the stated citation date.\n\n- Section 3.3.3 states Claude Sonnet 4.6 is used as the LLM judge. This model version should be verified for existence and the evaluation date noted, as model versions evolve rapidly.\n\n- The limitations section acknowledges \"no online evaluation\" but this deserves more prominence given the title's \"Intervention\" framing.\n\n### 6. Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | 2/4 | The headline result (0.967) is against silver labels with known circularity concerns. The gold validation (0.839) is underpowered. Stride-1 autocorrelation invalidates statistical tests. Multiple planned experiments are missing. The deployable configuration (2-LF) has near-zero coverage on the target agent. |\n| **Presentation** | 3/4 | Well-written with clear structure and commendable transparency about circularity. However, the framing prioritizes the inflated silver-label result over the gold-validated one, and the title promises intervention analysis that is absent. |\n| **Contribution** | 2/4 | The weak supervision framework for agent monitoring and the negative label model finding are useful. However, without the early termination simulation, calibration analysis, or online deployment results, the \"Detection to Intervention\" narrative is incomplete. The practical deployability claim is undermined by the 2-LF coverage gap. |\n| **Overall** | 3/6 (Weak Reject) | The paper tackles an important and timely problem with a reasonable methodology and commendable transparency about its limitations. However, the gap between the headline claims (0.967 F1, intervention capabilities) and the validated evidence (0.839 gold F1, no intervention results, underpowered tests with unaddressed autocorrelation) is too large for acceptance. The missing experiments -- particularly early termination, calibration, and stride sensitivity analysis -- leave critical claims unsubstantiated. A revision incorporating these experiments, correcting statistical tests for dependence, and reframing around gold-validated results would strengthen the paper substantially. |\n| **Confidence** | 4/5 | I have read the paper carefully including all appendices, cross-referenced the experiment design, and checked statistical methodology. My main uncertainty is whether early termination results exist elsewhere (e.g., in code/artifacts) but were simply omitted from the manuscript. |"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: addc08c3b1bd8de25 (use SendMessage with to: 'addc08c3b1bd8de25' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 48188\ntool_uses: 12\nduration_ms: 118262</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8ba2873e-73c0-42d5-b773-116ccfe0b545","uuid":"93099249-14b6-4d76-8cff-52c27fb93c82","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:22:19.445Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"From Detection to Intervention: Structural Progress Monitoring for Compute-Efficient LLM Agent Execution\"\n\nThe paper presents a framework for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories and using it for early termination. The approach:\n1. Designs 3 labeling functions (LFs): command repetition (cmd_rep), file diff, outcome backpropagation (OB)\n2. Generates silver labels via majority vote of LFs\n3. Trains a Gradient Boosted Tree (GB) on 30 structural features, plus neural embedding probes\n4. Evaluates cross-agent transfer (train on SWE-Agent, test on OpenHands zero-shot)\n5. Validates against 505-window human gold set (3 annotators, Fleiss κ=0.39)\n6. Simulates early termination policies\n\n## Key Results\n- Cross-agent: Structural GB achieves 0.967 macro F1 on OH, outperforming best LLM judge by 18.4pp and zero-shot probes by 28-37pp\n- Gold validation: GB 0.839 vs MV 0.798 (p<0.001)\n- Conf=1 subset (N=282): GB 0.718 vs MV 0.709 (p=0.035) — suggestive but underpowered\n- Human ceiling ~0.849; GB at 0.839 within 1pp\n- Early termination k=3: saves 23% compute, preserves 91% task success\n- 2-LF deployable config: only 2.8% conf≥2 coverage on OH (vs 18.7% on SWE-Agent)\n- Calibration: gold ECE 0.082 vs silver 0.045 — overconfidence from circular training\n- Feature importance: 52.8% LF-correlated, 32.1% residual\n- Stride sensitivity: stride 1 autocorrelation 0.72 makes bootstrap CIs anticonservative\n- TLM negative result: three temporal label models fail to beat MV\n- Compute waste: ~32% of steps occur after stagnation onset\n\n## Experiment Design Reference\nThe review context lists these imagined experiments with expected results:\n1. Early Termination Simulation — k=3 best operating point, 23% savings, 91% success preserved\n2. Compute Waste Audit — ~32% waste, concentrated in unresolved trajectories\n3. Cost-Effectiveness Pareto — structural AUPF 0.847, oracle 0.952, random 0.643, efficiency ratio 0.66\n4. LLM-on-OH Matched Comparison — best LLM 0.781, structural 0.965, gap 18.4pp\n5. Conf=1 Gold Error Decomposition — net GB advantage 10 windows, McNemar p=0.035\n6. Cross-Agent Probe BGE Zero-Shot — 0.685 F1, gap 28.2pp to structural\n7. Cross-Agent Probe E5 Zero-Shot — 0.632 F1, gap 33.5pp\n8. 2-LF Coverage Analysis — OH conf≥2 only 2.8% coverage\n9. Calibration Analysis — gold ECE nearly doubles vs silver\n10. Gold Power Analysis — min detectable effect at 80% power is 6.2pp\n11. Human Ceiling Estimate — mean annotator F1 0.849\n12. Stride Sensitivity — stride 1 autocorrelation 0.72\n13. Feature Importance — 52.8% LF-correlated\n14. Trajectory Visualization — 3 example trajectories\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (methodological and experimental)\n3. **Weaknesses** (methodological and experimental) — be specific, cite tables/sections\n4. **Questions for Authors** (3-5 specific questions)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Scores**: Soundness (1-4), Presentation (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Overall (1-6), Confidence (1-5)\n\nKey things to evaluate:\n- Is the circular evaluation concern adequately addressed? (classifier trained on LF-derived labels, evaluated partly on same)\n- Is the 0.967 cross-agent F1 meaningful given circularity?\n- Is the gold set (N=505) adequate? Power analysis shows underpowered for key comparisons\n- Is the early termination simulation realistic? (offline, not online deployment)\n- Is the 2-LF deployable claim credible given 2.8% OH coverage?\n- Are the statistical tests appropriate?\n- Is the stride 1 autocorrelation (0.72) adequately handled?\n- Does the paper cover all experiments from the design? Check coverage against the 14 experiments listed above.\n\nWrite a thorough, fair review. Be specific with citations to tables and sections.","agentId":"addc08c3b1bd8de25","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me compile the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"From Detection to Intervention: Structural Progress Monitoring for Compute-Efficient LLM Agent Execution\"\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper studies stagnation detection in LLM coding agents using a weak supervision framework with three labeling functions (command repetition, file diff, outcome backpropagation) to generate silver labels over 205K+ sliding windows from SWE-Agent and OpenHands trajectories. The authors compare structural features (30-dim, trained with GB/RF/LR), a GRU-based neural embedding probe, and an LLM-as-judge baseline across in-domain and cross-agent transfer settings. The main claim is that lightweight structural features (no GPU, <5ms) achieve 0.967 macro-F1 in zero-data cross-agent transfer, outperforming neural probes by 36.9pp and the best LLM judge by 16.8pp, while a 505-window gold set partially validates these findings.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. Well-structured circularity analysis.** The paper is unusually transparent about the circular evaluation problem inherent in training on LF-derived labels and evaluating with LF-correlated features. Section 4 (circular evaluation caveat), Section 5.1 (circular evaluation subsection), and Section 5.2 (feature overlap ablation) systematically decompose this concern. The instance isolation experiment (Table A.5, delta < 0.1pp) is a particularly clean control.\n\n**S2. Cross-agent transfer is the right evaluation.** The SWE-Agent-to-OpenHands zero-shot transfer design is well-motivated and partially breaks circularity: cmd_rep fires on 65.5% of SWE-Agent windows but only 5.4% on OH, so the GB model cannot simply memorize LF firing patterns. The feature overlap ablation (Table A.7) showing that LF-overlapping features *alone* achieve 0.977 cross-agent F1 (exceeding the full 30-feature model) while residual features collapse to 0.566 is informative.\n\n**S3. Negative results are valuable.** The temporal label model results (Section 4.6, Appendix Table A.6) are a genuine contribution. Showing that five label models spanning temporal and generative approaches all fail to beat majority vote in the 3-LF regime is practically useful for practitioners. The root-cause analysis (insufficient inter-LF conflict, only 8 possible voting patterns) is convincing.\n\n**S4. Honest deployment trade-off framing.** The paper does not oversell: it clearly states that the probe surpasses structural features with fine-tuning (0.976 vs 0.967 on all windows) and delineates when each approach is appropriate. The data scaling curve (Figure 2) showing structural classifiers saturating at 10% training data versus the probe not plateauing at 100% is an informative practical finding.\n\n**S5. Thorough ablation design.** The paper includes encoder ablation (3 backbones, Appendix Table A.1), window size ablation (Table A.2), multi-seed stability (Table A.3), leave-one-group-out feature ablation (Table A.7), and prevalence-matched cmd_rep ablation (Table 2). The ablation coverage is above average for the venue.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. The 0.967 cross-agent F1 is fundamentally against silver labels, and the paper's framing obscures this.** The headline result (0.967 macro-F1) is evaluated against OH silver labels produced by the same family of labeling functions used to train the model. While the paper argues this partially breaks circularity because LF firing patterns differ across agents, the evaluation target remains LF-derived. The gold set (N=505, macro-F1=0.839) is the only non-circular evidence, and it shows a 12.8pp gap from the headline number. The abstract and introduction lead with \"0.967\" and present \"0.839\" as secondary validation, but from a soundness perspective the relationship should be inverted: the gold-validated performance is 0.839, and readers should treat this as the primary result. This framing choice inflates the perceived contribution.\n\n**W2. The gold set is underpowered for the claims it needs to support.** N=505 with Fleiss kappa=0.39 (fair agreement) is thin for validating a system that claims practical deployment readiness. The paper's own context mentions a power analysis showing minimum detectable effect of 6.2pp at 80% power -- yet the GB vs MV gold difference is only 4.2pp. The conf=1 subset comparison (GB 0.718 vs MV 0.709, p=0.035, Section 4.7 context) is described as \"suggestive but underpowered\" -- this is honest but undermines the gold set's ability to independently validate the core claims. More critically, probe models are evaluated on a SWE-only subset (N~335) due to missing OH embeddings, making the gold comparison between structural and probe methods on different subsets (structural on N=505, probe on N~335), which is methodologically unclean.\n\n**W3. Multiple experiments from the design are missing from the paper.** Cross-referencing the 14-experiment design with the paper's content reveals significant gaps:\n- **Experiment 1 (Early Termination Simulation)**: Not present in any section. The paper title promises \"Intervention\" and mentions \"early termination\" in the introduction and limitations, but no simulation results (k=3 operating point, 23% savings, 91% success preservation) appear anywhere. This is a major gap given the title.\n- **Experiment 2 (Compute Waste Audit)**: Not present. The 32% waste figure appears nowhere in the paper.\n- **Experiment 3 (Cost-Effectiveness Pareto)**: Not present. No Pareto analysis, no AUPF metric.\n- **Experiment 9 (Calibration Analysis)**: Not present. The gold ECE 0.082 vs silver 0.045 result and overconfidence discussion are absent.\n- **Experiment 10 (Gold Power Analysis)**: Not present as a formal analysis, though the underpowered nature is mentioned in passing.\n- **Experiment 11 (Human Ceiling Estimate)**: Not present. Mean annotator F1 of 0.849 is not reported.\n- **Experiment 12 (Stride Sensitivity)**: Not present. The stride-1 autocorrelation of 0.72 and its anticonservative effect on bootstrap CIs is not discussed. This is a serious statistical concern -- if stride-1 creates high autocorrelation, all bootstrap CIs throughout the paper may be anticonservative, and this is never acknowledged.\n- **Experiment 14 (Trajectory Visualization)**: Not present.\n\nThe absence of the early termination simulation is particularly damaging given the title's promise of \"Intervention.\" Without it, the paper is purely about detection with no validated intervention path.\n\n**W4. The 2-LF deployable configuration claim is undermined by the paper's own data.** The cmd_rep ablation (Table 2) shows that removing cmd_rep drops macro-F1 by 29.4pp. The paper notes that cmd_rep fires on only 5.4% of OH windows. The experiment design context indicates 2-LF conf>=2 coverage is only 2.8% on OH vs 18.7% on SWE-Agent. If outcome backpropagation (lambda_3) requires task outcome unavailable at inference (acknowledged in limitations), the deployable configuration is actually 2-LF, which is catastrophically weak. The paper does not adequately address what a realistic deployment looks like without lambda_3.\n\n**W5. Statistical tests may be invalid due to stride-1 autocorrelation.** The experiment design mentions stride-1 autocorrelation of 0.72. With overlapping windows (stride 1, window size 5), consecutive windows share 4 of 5 actions, creating massive dependence. All bootstrap confidence intervals, McNemar tests, and p-values throughout the paper assume (at least approximately) independent observations. An autocorrelation of 0.72 would dramatically inflate test statistics and narrow CIs. The paper never discusses this issue, yet it affects every statistical test reported (paired bootstrap in Table 1, McNemar in Table 2, gold set comparisons in Table A.8).\n\n**W6. The LLM-as-judge comparison is unfair.** The best LLM judge (five-shot-CoT, 0.799) is evaluated in-domain on SWE-Agent, while the structural GB (0.967) is evaluated cross-agent on OH -- both against silver labels. The paper explicitly notes \"the structural advantage holds even comparing the harder cross-agent setting against the LLM's easier in-domain regime\" (Section 4.2), but this is misleading: the LLM is evaluated on a different test set with different characteristics. A fair comparison would evaluate both methods on the same test set. The GPT-5.5 result has 22.4% parsing failures (408/1824 windows), and computing F1 only on parseable outputs introduces selection bias.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** Given that the title promises \"Intervention\" and mentions early termination repeatedly, where are the early termination simulation results? The experiment design includes a k=3 operating point saving 23% compute while preserving 91% task success. Was this experiment conducted? If so, why is it absent from the paper? If not, the title should be revised.\n\n**Q2.** How do you address the stride-1 autocorrelation (0.72) in your statistical tests? Consecutive windows share 4 of 5 actions, creating strong dependence. Have you verified that your bootstrap CIs remain valid under this dependence structure? A block bootstrap with block length >= 5 would be more appropriate. What happens to the GB vs MV gold-set p-value (reported as p<0.001) under a dependence-corrected test?\n\n**Q3.** What is the realistic deployable configuration? Lambda_3 requires task outcome (unavailable at inference). Lambda_1 fires on only 5.4% of OH windows. The 2-LF configuration has 2.8% conf>=2 coverage on OH. What stagnation detection coverage can a practitioner actually expect when deploying this system on a new agent without task outcomes?\n\n**Q4.** The gold set evaluates probe models on a SWE-only subset (N~335) but structural models on the full set (N=505). Why are OH embeddings missing? This prevents the most important comparison -- structural vs probe on the same gold examples including cross-agent transfer cases. Can you provide a scope-matched comparison?\n\n**Q5.** The feature overlap ablation shows LF-overlapping features alone achieve 0.977 cross-agent F1, exceeding the full model. This suggests residual features actively hurt cross-agent transfer. Have you tried training with only the 11 LF-overlapping features for deployment? What is the gold-set performance of this reduced model?\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The label polarity convention (0=stagnation, 1=progress) is counterintuitive for binary classification where the \"positive\" class is typically the phenomenon being detected. The error analysis (Appendix A.9) switches to \"stagnation-positive convention\" midway, creating confusion.\n\n- Table 1 footnote $^a$ notes that probe is evaluated on N=6,122 while structural models on N=150,652 (or N=43,376 for conf>=2). This sample size mismatch makes the comparison in the same table misleading without explicit caveat in the main text.\n\n- The paper uses \"coding agent\" and \"agent\" interchangeably but calls it \"cross-coding-agent transfer\" in a way that reads awkwardly. \"Cross-agent\" is clearer.\n\n- The Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B citation (zhang2025qwen3embedding) should be verified -- Qwen3 embedding models may not have existed under this name at the stated citation date.\n\n- Section 3.3.3 states Claude Sonnet 4.6 is used as the LLM judge. This model version should be verified for existence and the evaluation date noted, as model versions evolve rapidly.\n\n- The limitations section acknowledges \"no online evaluation\" but this deserves more prominence given the title's \"Intervention\" framing.\n\n### 6. Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Soundness** | 2/4 | The headline result (0.967) is against silver labels with known circularity concerns. The gold validation (0.839) is underpowered. Stride-1 autocorrelation invalidates statistical tests. Multiple planned experiments are missing. The deployable configuration (2-LF) has near-zero coverage on the target agent. |\n| **Presentation** | 3/4 | Well-written with clear structure and commendable transparency about circularity. However, the framing prioritizes the inflated silver-label result over the gold-validated one, and the title promises intervention analysis that is absent. |\n| **Contribution** | 2/4 | The weak supervision framework for agent monitoring and the negative label model finding are useful. However, without the early termination simulation, calibration analysis, or online deployment results, the \"Detection to Intervention\" narrative is incomplete. The practical deployability claim is undermined by the 2-LF coverage gap. |\n| **Overall** | 3/6 (Weak Reject) | The paper tackles an important and timely problem with a reasonable methodology and commendable transparency about its limitations. However, the gap between the headline claims (0.967 F1, intervention capabilities) and the validated evidence (0.839 gold F1, no intervention results, underpowered tests with unaddressed autocorrelation) is too large for acceptance. The missing experiments -- particularly early termination, calibration, and stride sensitivity analysis -- leave critical claims unsubstantiated. A revision incorporating these experiments, correcting statistical tests for dependence, and reframing around gold-validated results would strengthen the paper substantially. |\n| **Confidence** | 4/5 | I have read the paper carefully including all appendices, cross-referenced the experiment design, and checked statistical methodology. My main uncertainty is whether early termination results exist elsewhere (e.g., in code/artifacts) but were simply omitted from the manuscript. |"}],"totalDurationMs":118262,"totalTokens":48188,"totalToolUseCount":12,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3110,"cache_read_input_tokens":41659,"output_tokens":3418,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3110},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3418,"cache_read_input_tokens":41659,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3110,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3110,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":10,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
