{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PBYSqYBg5jdjh46BEfR9hq","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/paper/main.bbl:151:\\newblock Trajad: Trajectory anomaly detection for trustworthy llm agents.\ndocs/paper/main.bbl:286:\\newblock Aligning agents via planning: A benchmark for trajectory-level reward\ndocs/paper/acl.sty:236:\\def\\subsection{\\@startsection{subsection}{2}{\\z@}{-1.8ex plus\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl_latex.tex:180:\\subsection{Footnotes}\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl_latex.tex:184:\\subsection{Tables and figures}\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl_latex.tex:246:\\subsection{Hyperlinks}\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl_latex.tex:254:\\subsection{Citations}\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl_latex.tex:286:\\subsection{References}\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl_latex.tex:308:\\subsection{Equations}\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl_latex.tex:322:\\subsection{Appendices}\ndocs/paper/_acl_template/acl.sty:236:\\def\\subsection{\\@startsection{subsection}{2}{\\z@}{-1.8ex plus\ndocs/paper/sections/analysis.tex:4:\\subsection{Circular Evaluation and Its Implications}\ndocs/paper/sections/analysis.tex:14:\\subsection{Feature Overlap Ablation}\ndocs/paper/sections/analysis.tex:22:\\subsection{Additional Analysis}\ndocs/paper/sections/related_work.tex:21:Cycle detectors \\citep{george2025cycledetection,pipis2025reasoningloop} target structural repetition but miss semantically diverse stagnation; \\citet{liu2026trajad} train trajectory verifiers for broader anomaly detection.\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:17:\\subsection{Experimental Setup}\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:23:Splits are at the trajectory level (70/15/15\\%); evaluation uses conf${\\geq}$2: $N{=}1{,}824$ (SWE-Agent) and $N{=}43{,}376$ (OH).\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:36:\\subsection{Main Results}\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:73:\\subsection{Cross-Agent Transfer}\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:91:\\subsection{Robustness Analysis}\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:106:\\subsection{Labeling Function Temporal Dynamics}\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:113:\\subsection{Label Quality and cmd\\_rep Coverage}\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:138:\\subsection{Temporal Label Models}\ndocs/paper/sections/experiments.tex:145:\\subsection{Gold Set Validation}\ndocs/paper/sections/method.tex:16:\\subsection{Problem Formulation}\ndocs/paper/sections/method.tex:19:Given an agent trajectory $T = (a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n)$ where each $a_t$ is an action (e.g., a shell command, file edit, or API call), we partition it into overlapping sliding windows $w_i = (a_i, \\ldots, a_{i+k-1})$ with window size $k{=}5$ and stride 1.\ndocs/paper/sections/method.tex:24:Compared to trajectory-level labeling, windows provide the granularity needed to pinpoint \\emph{when} stagnation begins and ends within a single task attempt, enabling downstream interventions such as early termination or strategy switching.\ndocs/paper/sections/method.tex:27:\\subsection{Weak Supervision Pipeline}\ndocs/paper/sections/method.tex:69:\\subsection{Detection Methods}\ndocs/paper/sections/method.tex:105:\\subsection{Temporal Label Models}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:5:\\subsection{Encoder Ablation}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:25:\\subsection{Window Size Ablation}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:45:\\subsection{Multi-Seed Stability}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:65:\\subsection{Cross-Agent Label Quality and Few-Shot Transfer}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:97:\\subsection{Structural Feature Definitions}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:110:1  & num\\_steps             & Trajectory stats    & --- \\\\\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:111:2  & num\\_actions           & Trajectory stats    & --- \\\\\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:112:3  & num\\_observations      & Trajectory stats    & --- \\\\\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:113:4  & action\\_ratio          & Trajectory stats    & --- \\\\\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:139:30 & is\\_short\\_trajectory  & Trajectory stats    & $\\lambda_3$ \\\\\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:147:\\subsection{Instance Isolation}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:168:\\subsection{Extended LLM-as-Judge Evaluation}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:195:\\subsection{Temporal Label Model Specifications}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:223:\\subsection{Label Quality: Extended Analysis}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:241:\\subsection{Temporal Label Model Results}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:280:\\paragraph{Root cause: insufficient inter-LF conflict.}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:285:\\subsection{Label Model Failure Analysis}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:302:\\subsection{Error Analysis}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:316:Errors concentrate in trajectory midpoints (10.2\\% error rate vs.\\ 6.5\\% at endpoints), where the boundary between exploration and stagnation is most ambiguous.\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:331:\\subsection{Outcome Backpropagation as Distant Supervision}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:343:\\subsection{Labeling Function Temporal Dynamics}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:372:\\subsection{Gold Set Validation Protocol}\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:375:We sample 650 windows stratified along two axes---\\emph{confidence} (balanced across conf${=}1$, conf${=}2$, and conf${=}3$ strata) and \\emph{trajectory position} (early, middle, and late thirds)---for non-circular evaluation.\ndocs/paper/sections/appendix.tex:422:\\subsection{Feature Overlap Ablation Table}\ndocs/paper/sections/introduction.tex:5:As these deployments scale, a critical failure mode has emerged that is qualitatively different from crashes or syntax errors: the agent \\emph{stagnates}---continuing to produce actions that appear purposeful while making no meaningful progress toward the task goal.\ndocs/paper/sections/introduction.tex:21:Three labeling functions provide complementary noisy votes: command repetition ($\\lambda_1$) targets repetitive behavior, file diff delta ($\\lambda_2$) targets output productivity, and outcome backpropagation ($\\lambda_3$) propagates task-level outcomes backward through the trajectory.\ndocs/paper/sections/introduction.tex:23:Our window-level formulation ($k{=}5$ consecutive actions, stride 1) captures short-range temporal patterns---such as repeated failures followed by strategy changes---that step-level approaches cannot express, while providing the granularity to pinpoint \\emph{when} stagnation begins and ends within a trajectory, enabling targeted interventions.\ndocs/paper/references.bib:27:  title   = {Aligning Agents via Planning: A Benchmark for Trajectory-Level Reward Modeling},\ndocs/paper/references.bib:237:  title   = {TrajAD: Trajectory Anomaly Detection for Trustworthy LLM Agents},"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NdDgBm8ZE2PUmfzpt5RUcR","session_id":"8ba2873e-73c0-42d5-b773-116ccfe0b545","uuid":"e0663e9c-05ea-4dff-8442-ce7f13070856","timestamp":"2026-05-16T03:24:45.194Z"}
